from exits import *
sys.path.insert(0,str(HERE.parents[1]/'work'))
from research_reporting import table,document,paths,escape,plt
def main():
    out=HERE/'reports';p=pd.read_csv(out/'performance.csv');diag=pd.read_csv(out/'account_diagnostics.csv');status=read(out/'status.json');qa=read(out/'qa.json');r=pd.read_csv(out/'daily_returns.csv.gz',index_col=0,parse_dates=True);labels=dict(none='No exit overlay',fixed5='5% fixed stop',trailing8='8% trailing stop',bracket5_10='5% stop / 10% profit',vol2sigma='Volatility stop',tree='Continuation tree');paths(r,list(ARMS),labels,out/'exit_paths.png','Position exits on the same correlation-aware +0.50-beta benchmark')
    from sklearn.tree import plot_tree
    model=pickle.loads((HERE/'models/tree_2022.pkl').read_bytes());fig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(12,6));plot_tree(model,feature_names=FEATURES,filled=True,rounded=True,precision=4,fontsize=10,ax=ax,impurity=False);ax.set_title('Tree applied during 2022 — trained only on mature outcomes before 2022');fig.tight_layout();fig.savefig(out/'tree_2022.png',dpi=150);plt.close(fig)
    wide=[]
    for a in ARMS:
        z=p.loc[p.arm.eq(a)].set_index('period');wide.append({'Exit rule':labels[a],'2012–23 CAGR':z.loc['primary','cagr'],'2007–23 CAGR':z.loc['full','cagr'],'Full max DD':z.loc['full','max_drawdown'],'Full Sharpe':z.loc['full','sharpe'],'2022 return':z.loc['stress_2022','net_return']})
    w=pd.DataFrame(wide);pct=['2012–23 CAGR','2007–23 CAGR','Full max DD','2022 return'];base=p.loc[p.arm.eq('none')].set_index('period');improved=p.loc[p.period.eq('full')&p.arm.ne('none')&p.max_drawdown.gt(base.loc['full','max_drawdown']),'arm'].tolist();body='<p class="note">Completed exploratory research. All six accounts use the same stock forecasts, source data, fees and funding constraints. Stops are observed at a close and filled at the next close; these results do not describe resting intraday stop orders. No model promotion.</p>'
    body+='<p>Every exit rule reduced the observed full-history maximum drawdown, and every rule also reduced CAGR. The volatility-scaled stop retained the most return: 8.32% CAGR versus 8.43%, with drawdown improving from 28.41% to 26.12% and Sharpe from 0.73 to 0.79. The tighter 5%/10% bracket cut drawdown to 16.96% but reduced CAGR to 5.36%. This favors the volatility-scaled version for further research, not automatic adoption.</p><p>Growth comparisons passing both block lengths after this study’s five-comparison Holm correction: '+escape(str(status['growth_holm_both_blocks']))+'. Drawdown differences are descriptive, and the same history has already been used in earlier research.</p>'+table(w,pct)
    body+='<img src="reports/exit_paths.png" alt="Full-history equity, drawdowns and 2022 cumulative returns for six exit rules">'
    body+='<h2>What was tested</h2><p>The benchmark is the frozen v36 fixed correlation-aware stock-selection model with the original 21-session forecast cadence and a +0.50 beta limit. It is independent of the simultaneously tested higher-beta variants. The no-overlay replay matches the previous ledger exactly.</p><p>The fixed stop uses a 5% adverse move from average executed entry price. The trailing stop uses an 8% adverse move from the best closing price since entry: a high for longs and a low for shorts. The bracket exits at a 5% loss or 10% gain. The volatility stop uses twice the stock’s entry-time 63-session volatility, scaled to 21 sessions, with the distance limited to 3%–20%. All prices are adjusted total-return wealth prices, so these are research thresholds rather than raw-price broker instructions.</p><p>A stopped name stays excluded until the next original forecast. Same-side additions update average entry basis; partial reductions preserve it. Exits bypass the usual trading buffer, pay actual modeled fees and use the next available account execution. Missing or provisional marks cannot trigger stops. The remaining portfolio is reprojected to satisfy beta and correlation-factor constraints, subject to its remaining gross budget and the same 12% forecast-risk cap; other positions can therefore change too.</p>'
    body+='<h2>Exposure and trading effects</h2>'
    d=diag.loc[diag.period.eq('full'),['arm','triggers','long_exits','short_exits','mean_target_beta','mean_actual_beta','mean_gross','mean_cash_fraction','submissions']].copy();d.arm=d.arm.map(labels);body+=table(d,['mean_cash_fraction'])
    cost=p.loc[p.period.eq('full'),['arm','volatility','turnover_per_year','cost_per_year','average_net_exposure','spy_beta','annual_long_net_contribution','annual_short_net_contribution']].copy();cost.arm=cost.arm.map(labels);body+=table(cost,['volatility','cost_per_year','annual_long_net_contribution','annual_short_net_contribution'])
    body+='<p>A lower drawdown can reflect less exposure as well as better exit timing. The 5% fixed stop reduced average estimated beta from 0.495 to 0.397 and gross exposure from 2.22 to 1.80; modeled trading costs increased from 0.59% to 0.95% per year. The volatility stop retained beta around 0.474 and gross around 2.15, with costs around 0.71%. These exposure and cost changes help explain the return tradeoff. The post-exit portfolio is not automatically scaled back to its original leverage.</p>'
    opportunity=pd.read_csv(out/'exit_opportunity.csv');z=opportunity.loc[opportunity.period.eq('full'),['arm','side','triggers','coverage','mean_signed_h21_after_exit','positive_fraction']].copy();z.arm=z.arm.map(labels);z.side=z.side.map({1:'Long',-1:'Short'});body+='<h2>Did exited positions recover?</h2><p>This descriptive check looks at the underlying stock’s signed return from the next-close exit price through the following 21 sessions. A positive mean indicates foregone gains after exiting; a negative mean indicates further adverse moves avoided. It is unweighted and does not include the portfolio’s hedge changes or subsequent re-entry, so it is not an attribution of realized portfolio profit. These future labels are used only for this evaluation.</p>'+table(z,['coverage','mean_signed_h21_after_exit','positive_fraction'])
    body+='<h2>The payoff-tree experiment</h2><p>Each annual depth-two regression tree estimates the advantage of holding another 21 sessions versus exiting next close. It uses only completed past labels from the prior five years, sampled every fifth session from actual baseline position states. Inputs are side, current entry-relative P&amp;L, trailing drawdown, position age, entry and current volatility, and forecast position size. Positions at least five sessions old exit when predicted holding advantage is below −0.20%. The 2007–2011 warm-up uses the fixed 5% stop.</p><p>This is a continuation-value proxy, not a full optimal-stopping solution: it does not recursively value all future exit/re-entry choices or learn from the state distribution induced by its own policy. It cannot establish globally optimal stop or profit zones. The 2022 model mainly separates side and volatility; both short leaves predict negative continuation value, so its behavior also tests shrinking the short book.</p><img src="reports/tree_2022.png" alt="Pre-2022 continuation tree with side and volatility splits"><p><small>Leaf values are expected 21-session signed returns as decimals; sigma21 is the stock’s estimated 21-session volatility. Exiting also requires the age and −0.20% threshold described above.</small></p>'
    body+='<h2>Research basis</h2><p><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S138641811300030X">Kaminski and Lo, When Do Stop-Loss Rules Stop Losses?</a> show that the usefulness of stops depends on return dynamics; their random-walk setting and momentum setting give different conclusions. This motivates evaluating stops jointly with the strategy and re-entry policy.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.03960">Leung and Zhang, Optimal Trading with a Trailing Stop</a> derive an optimal liquidation rule combining a trailing stop and sell limit under a specified diffusion model. It motivates testing a profit-taking boundary but does not supply empirically optimal thresholds for our portfolio.</p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.07211">Ciocan and Mišić, Interpretable Optimal Stopping</a> learn interpretable stopping policies represented by trees. Our shallow continuation regression is a simpler hypothesis, not a replication of their direct tree-policy optimization.</p>'
    body+='<h2>Validation and limits</h2><p>Exact baseline replay, next-close timing and adverse-gap tests, blocked-name exclusions, account arithmetic, mature annual training labels, source hashes and post-exit portfolio constraints passed. '+str(qa['blocked_target_vectors_checked'])+' post-exit target vectors and '+str(qa['stop_trigger_records'])+' trigger records were checked. Source valuations remain conditional on the inherited data audit; zero borrow fees and cash interest are unchanged. Borrow-cost sensitivity is provided separately. Full-history results include the fixed-stop warm-up for the tree.</p>'
    links=['performance.csv','annual.csv','account_diagnostics.csv','trigger_annual.csv','exit_opportunity.csv','paired_growth.csv','borrow_sensitivity.csv','tree_training.json','qa.json','status.json'];body+='<p>'+ ' · '.join(f'<a href="reports/{n}">{escape(n)}</a>' for n in links)+'</p><p><a href="protocol.json">Frozen protocol</a> · <a href="engine_changes.diff">Account adapter diff</a> · <a href="../v37_adaptive_correlation_beta/index.html">Higher-beta study</a></p>';document('Do position exits reduce the portfolio’s drawdowns?',body,HERE/'index.html')
if __name__=='__main__':main()
